    "md5": "0e3da7e1b250240c681e0440c0d60544",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1724,
    "chunk_text": "War on drugs: Some concerns and queries\n\nThe progress of the ongoing anti-narcotics drive was reviewed at the second meeting of the steering council of the \u2018Nation United\u2019 campaign, at the Presidential Secretariat, on Tuesday, with President Anura Kumara Dissanayake presiding. The discussion was dominated by some council members\u2019 concerns about an allegation that a part of the narcotics taken into custody ended up in the hands of drug dealers. The issue was raised by a former PNB (Police Narcotics Bureau) officer-turned-musician, Sangeeth Wijesuriya, who is an ardent supporter of the NPP. Their concerns are not unfounded. In 2020, the then Police Spokesperson SSP Jaliya Senaratne revealed that 18 PNB officers had been arrested for allegedly passing drugs in the PNB custody to drug dealers. Two Government Analyst\u2019s Department employees who doubled as drug peddlers were also arrested in that year. This shows how compromised some of those tasked with battling the scourge of narcotics are.\n\nWijesuriya said that eight kilos of narcotics, taken into custody some years back, had ended up as kurakkan flour, of all things. The wealthy drug Mafia has spread its tentacles over vital state institutions, and some public officials are in its pay. This has made the task of ridding the country of narcotics even more difficult.\n\nThe focus of anti-narcotics operations conducted at sea is usually on the smuggling of dangerous drugs into this country. There is reason to believe that Sri Lanka has become a narcotics hub, and most of the drugs smuggled in go undetected. Experts argue that some of the drugs brought here are smuggled out to other countries. This dimension of the problem deserves more attention."
  },
  {
