    "md5": "e7479053470f895f1fe31ae31e2b5f22",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1794,
    "chunk_text": "The focus of anti-narcotics operations conducted at sea is usually on the smuggling of dangerous drugs into this country. There is reason to believe that Sri Lanka has become a narcotics hub, and most of the drugs smuggled in go undetected. Experts argue that some of the drugs brought here are smuggled out to other countries. This dimension of the problem deserves more attention.\n\nSupporting an argument put forth by Wijesuriya, the President said the Magistrates visited crime scenes and conducted investigations, and thereafter the bodies of the victims were released for funerals. Narcotics taken into custody could be disposed of in a similar manner, he argued. However, this analogy does not hold in all respects. Funerals for the victims of crimes are allowed on the condition that their bodies are buried so that they could be exhumed if necessary. Care must be taken to ensure that there will be no loopholes in the laws to be made to enable the on-site destruction of drugs. Even the Constitution of this country is not without flaws.\n\nPraising the success of the ongoing anti-narcotics operations, Wijesuriya, who was in the vanguard of the NPP\u2019s election campaigns, claimed that under previous governments only several kilos of narcotics had been taken into custody. However, anti-narcotics operations yielded large stocks of drugs in the past as well. The NPP politicians also claim that the drug busting operations had not been successful before last year\u2019s regime change. Such propagandistic claims are an indictment of the PNB, the STF, the Navy, the Coast Guard, the Customs, etc., engaged in combating the drug menace, for their implication is that those institutions succumbed to political pressure and did not carry out their duties and functions properly in the past."
  },
