    "chunk_text": "Scandals, probes and waiting games\n\nCommittees and commissions come in handy for a government embroiled in scandals and desperate to play for time. They also help obfuscate issues under public scrutiny and open escape routes for the culprits with direct links to the ruling party. A Parliamentary Select Committee (PSC) is the optimal choice for a government struggling to stave off the adverse political impact of a scandal. Successive governments have misused the PSC process to further their interests.\n\nThe NPP government has recently decided to appoint two PSCs, one on the delayed Provincial Council (PC) polls and the other on the controversial release of 323 red-flagged containers without Customs inspection from the Colombo Port in January 2025. The first PSC is an attempt to delay the PC polls until the completion of the delimitation of electoral boundaries instead of amending the PC Elections Act to allow the PC polls to be held under the Proportional Representation system. The government members of the second PSC have been defending the NPP vis-\u00e0-vis damning allegations of wrongdoing against it.\n\nOne may consider it too early to prognosticate on how the \u2018container PSC\u2019 will conduct its investigation, which is yet to get underway. But it is doubtful whether the NPP government or a probe committee, dominated by its members, will proceed with an open mind to get at the truth; the container scandal has the potential to be the undoing of the current dispensation. There\u2019s the rub.\n\nIt is only wishful thinking that any investigation conducted by a committee headed by a government MP into an allegation against one of the ruling party heavyweights will be impartial. Expecting such a probe to help bring a wrongdoer to justice is no less ill-advised than \u2018seeking the help of a female clairvoyant to catch a thief who happens to be her own son\u2019, as the old local saying goes."
