    "md5": "f7b5b373af2643eccd388a3d9d3ab33a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1769,
    "chunk_text": "Needed: Action not rhetoric\n\nThe NPP MPs and their Opposition counterparts spoke at length, in Parliament on Thursday, to demonstrate what they made out to be their disaster management skills. The Opposition sought to teach the government how to handle the current disaster situation more effectively, and the latter claimed that it was on top of the situation, and rescue and relief programmes were successfully underway.\n\nGoing by the arguments and counterarguments put forth by the government and the Opposition in Parliament, they were quarrelling over what the state had to do for the people affected by disasters. Neither of them asked what the political parties dependent on the public for survival could do for the people in distress.\n\nLast week, the SLPP brought thousands of its supporters to a political rally in Nugegoda despite the inclement weather and boasted of having staged a very successful show of strength. They must be among those who are crying out for help. The SJB has said it will hold a political rally early next year, and the JVP/NPP has bragged that it is capable of staging a much bigger show of strength than the one held by the SLPP in Nugegoda. All these parties have held huge political events with the participation of tens of thousands of people.\n\nAn opportunity has now presented itself for the people\u2019s representatives, in both Parliament and the local government institutions, to show that they care for the people who pay through the nose to maintain them and vote for them generously. The least they can do for the public at this juncture is to mobilise their rank and file, whose numerical strengths they often brag about, in the disaster-stricken parts of the country and assist in the ongoing rescue and relief operations."
  },
