    "md5": "128e0a16f1a14848211f26a4a90d8d46",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2005,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka\u2019s history is replete with instances of floods, landslides, storms and other such disasters. Climate change is usually blamed for these adverse weather events. But in this country weather disasters have taken a turn for the worse over the last few decades, with death tolls rising alarmingly, due to unbridled human activities harmful to the environment, such as the ever increasing encroachment on forests and wetlands, illegal sand mining and deforestation caused by illicit felling, agricultural expansion and unplanned growth of settlements. These causative factors have gone unaddressed.\n\nDuring Thursday\u2019s parliamentary sitting, the Opposition sought to gain political mileage by criticising the government\u2019s disaster response and belittling its knowledge of the laws related to disaster management. The Opposition MPs themselves pathetically failed to respond to disaster situations properly, while in power, and are therefore without any moral right to treat the current administration to lectures on disaster management. While in opposition, the JVP/NPP leaders also had a field day, criticising the previous governments, whenever disasters occurred.\n\nDuring his Opposition days, President Anura Kumara Dissanayake would tear the previous governments into shreds for their policy blunders and failure to make timely interventions to prevent tragedies caused by weather disasters. Videos containing his past speeches on natural disasters and criticism of his political opponents in power to act on weather forecasts and adopt a scientific approach to disaster management have resurfaced in the digital realm. As Dr. Amarasinghe has pointed out, a window of opportunity has presented itself for him to act.\n\nThe success of any disaster prevention or disaster impact mitigation strategy hinges on the effective law enforcement and the implementation of policies in place to prevent disasters, manage their impacts, save lives, and look after victims and help them rebuild their lives."
  },
