    "chunk_size_bytes": 1623,
    "chunk_text": "These fairly recent White House chief female residents have been preceded by First Ladies who took pen to paper and were published. I was surprised that googling yielded14 previous wives of presidents who had been authors \u2013 mostly of memoirs, historical accounts and even children\u2019s books. Maybe also of causes they espoused such as the welfare of the disadvantaged. Two names stand out: Eleanor Roosevelt, and Laura Bush \u2013 their periods in the White House being 1933-1945 and 2001-2009 respectively.\n\nHillary Clinton\u2019s It takes a Village \u2026 deals with the importance of community and collective responsibility in child rearing and development. Inspired by an African proverb, she includes personal anecdotes of her family growing up; reports on child development; and examples of community models to argue that a village\u2019s support is crucial for children to grow into caring and resilient adults. Thus the main theme of the book is that raising a child requires the support of the community, not just their immediate family. This fact is well borne out by our experience. When we grew up \u2013 we meaning our generation and even the next \u2013 we lived in extended families. Many of our children\u2019s generation and modern families are confined to children and their parents only and thus the widespread use of drugs and children nowadays suffering mental instability. We felt so secure and happy with no gadgets of today, particularly social media nonexistent then.\n\nA tenth anniversary edition of It takes a Village was published featuring an updated introduction by the author and a separate picture book version."
