    "chunk_size_bytes": 2001,
    "chunk_text": "It reminded me of a Filipino productivity expert who once joked at a conference that when his presentation time was cut from 45 minutes to 20, he eliminated the content, removed the diagrams, and kept only the jokes. I did not dare adopt that strategy with this audience.\n\nThe study tour: a tale of two nations doing productivity right\n\nShortly afterward, a study visit was arranged for the Director General of NIBM, trade unionist Lesley Devendra, and myself. Our destinations were Malaysia and Singapore\u2014two countries that had transformed themselves in a matter of decades. The contrast with Sri Lanka was immediate and stark, offering lessons that remain relevant today.\n\nIn both countries, productivity did not float in bureaucratic limbo. It was anchored by political will at the highest level. Annual national themes were selected, cascaded through ministries, and executed with discipline.\n\nA central belief guided their approach: A productive public service is the foundation of a productive private sector. Efficient public institutions remove barriers, reduce delays, and create a conducive environment for businesses to thrive. In contrast, inefficient public institutions can strangle even the most vibrant private sector. This philosophy was evident everywhere we visited.\n\nOne of the most impressive elements in Malaysia was the role of its national productivity institution, the Malaysia Productivity Corporation (MPC)\u2014formerly the National Productivity Centre (NPC).\n\n\u00b7 1966: Established as NPC, focusing heavily on Japanese-inspired Quality Circles (QCs), worker involvement, and basic productivity tools.\n\nRebranded as MPC. Its mandate expanded to competitiveness, innovation, business process re-engineering, and benchmarking.\n\nMPC mainstreamed lean, regulatory reform, and national business process improvements.\n\n\u00b7 2020s: Emphasis on digital transformation and regulatory efficiency, aiming to position Malaysia among the world\u2019s top 12 most competitive economies by 2030."
  },
