    "md5": "bddfecacd29ec0d3fce3bd2b17629640",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1685,
    "chunk_text": "MPC mainstreamed lean, regulatory reform, and national business process improvements.\n\n\u00b7 2020s: Emphasis on digital transformation and regulatory efficiency, aiming to position Malaysia among the world\u2019s top 12 most competitive economies by 2030.\n\nDuring our discussions, we were told that all ministries and government departments were required to obtain ISO 9001 certification within that year. Curious, I asked: \u201cWhat happens if a department fails to get certified by the deadline?\u201d The answer was simple\u2014and rather telling: \u201cThat question is irrelevant. When Dr. Mahathir gives a deadline, it is met. Nobody dares to miss it.\u201d\n\nThat single line revealed more about the effectiveness of leadership than any management textbook.\n\nWe visited several organisations that had won the Prime Minister\u2019s Productivity Award. One memorable visit was to the newly opened North\u2013South Highway. Even the toll booth operators were trained in greeting techniques\u2014and taught how to smile.\n\nMy two colleagues insisted that I should take the smiling course, as they felt I looked \u201ctoo serious.\u201d I promised to consider it, though I suspect they were not entirely joking.\n\nSingapore, as always, operated at a different level of discipline and efficiency. Whether in government offices, transportation systems, or public spaces, the influence of thoughtful design and firm leadership was unmistakable.\n\nAt the time, T-shirts bearing the slogan \u201cSingapore is a Fine City\u201d were popular. It was a clever pun\u2014the city was indeed fine, but it was also full of fines:\n\nThis philosophy of behavioural discipline permeated daily life. A famous example awaited us at SPRING Singapore."
  },
  {
