    "md5": "0e2e290b2f8588f26ea5eb67331bcb60",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2034,
    "chunk_text": "At the time, T-shirts bearing the slogan \u201cSingapore is a Fine City\u201d were popular. It was a clever pun\u2014the city was indeed fine, but it was also full of fines:\n\nThis philosophy of behavioural discipline permeated daily life. A famous example awaited us at SPRING Singapore.\n\nHe explained that the sign was symbolic. No one had ever been fined. The Singaporean ethos is such that people obey rules because it is the right thing to do\u2014not because they fear detection.\n\nThen he added, with a touch of mischief: \u201cSri Lanka seems to have too many lawyers in powerful positions. Everything becomes about proof and evidence. That mindset slows a nation.\u201d It was his personal opinion, but whether one agrees or not, it certainly gives food for thought.\n\nSPRING Singapore \u2192 Enterprise Singapore: The Evolution of a Productivity Powerhouse\n\n\u00b7 1972: Establishment of the Singapore Productivity Board to modernise businesses and train the workforce.\n\nMerger with the Singapore Institute of Standards and Industrial Research (SISIR) to form the Singapore Productivity and Standards Board (PSB).\n\nPSB rebranded as SPRING Singapore, standing for Standards, Productivity, Innovation, and Growth\u2014a strategic combination under one umbrella.\n\n\u00b7 2018: Merger with IE Singapore to form Enterprise Singapore, a powerful institution overseeing standards, productivity, SME growth, and internationalisation.\n\nWhen we visited, the institution was still known as PSB. Yet its professionalism and clarity of mandate were already world-class. The Government ensured it had authority, funding, and governance. It was designed to shape the nation\u2019s future\u2014not merely react to it.\n\nReturning to Sri Lanka, I reflected on what we had seen. Malaysia had MPC. Singapore had PSB/SPRING. Both were statutory bodies with strong mandates, continuity, autonomy, and authority to drive national change.\n\nSri Lanka had the National Productivity Secretariat (NPS)\u2014a small unit buried within a ministry, lacking statutory power, authority, or permanence."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2025-11-29-islandlk-092d2430",
    "num": "islandlk-092d2430",
    "date_str": "2025-11-29",
    "description": "Continuing as an Advisor to the Government on Productivity Promotion",
    "url_metadata": "http://island.lk/continuing-as-an-advisor-to-the-government-on-productivity-promotion/",
