    "chunk_index": 4,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "ee7181322bb0c06deddf43c9b7fc0f83",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1978,
    "chunk_text": "Returning to Sri Lanka, I reflected on what we had seen. Malaysia had MPC. Singapore had PSB/SPRING. Both were statutory bodies with strong mandates, continuity, autonomy, and authority to drive national change.\n\nSri Lanka had the National Productivity Secretariat (NPS)\u2014a small unit buried within a ministry, lacking statutory power, authority, or permanence.\n\nThis passive neglect remains one of the quiet tragedies of Sri Lanka\u2019s long struggle for competitiveness.\n\nInside the Ministry, we slowly began building the identity of the NPS. With no legal recognition, we nonetheless adopted the name \u201cNational Productivity Secretariat.\u201d A Steering Committee guided us, but reaching consensus was often an uphill battle.\n\nA perfect example was the struggle to design a logo. One member proposed a tree full of fruits.\n\nAnother wanted the \u201c4Ms\u201d\u2014man, machine, money, and methods. The understanding of modern logo design was lacking. The debate dragged on for months.\n\nRealising the stalemate, I approached an advertising agency to create a few designs\u2014on a pro bono basis. I selected one, explained its symbolism to the Minister, and obtained his approval.\n\nBack at the Steering Committee, I simply said: \u201cThe Minister has approved this design. Shall we therefore adopt it?\u201d Unanimous approval. The logo still used today was born that way\u2014a reminder that sometimes leadership requires gentle manoeuvring rather than rigid process.\n\nThis chapter of my life yielded strong beliefs that remain with me\u2014and are especially relevant for Sri Lankan policy makers today.\n\nSingapore and Malaysia show that productivity cannot be delegated to minor units. It must be a national mission backed by the highest leadership.\n\nA small secretariat cannot transform a nation. Sri Lanka needs a statutory productivity authority with teeth, continuity, and independence.\n\nA slow or inefficient public sector is a hidden tax on every business. Productivity must start at the top."
  },
