    "md5": "df5c7303a801b1d18374066757bb8136",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1715,
    "chunk_text": "A small secretariat cannot transform a nation. Sri Lanka needs a statutory productivity authority with teeth, continuity, and independence.\n\nA slow or inefficient public sector is a hidden tax on every business. Productivity must start at the top.\n\nMalaysia enforced ISO certification. Singapore enforced Quality Circles. Clear deadlines, enforced with consequences, drive change.\n\nMy experience as Advisor strengthened my conviction that productivity is not a technical concept. It is a national attitude. Countries that rise\u2014Japan, Singapore, Malaysia\u2014treat productivity as a permanent national priority, supported by robust institutions and decisive leadership.\n\nSri Lanka has immense potential. But potential alone does not create prosperity. What we need is commitment, continuity, discipline, and the courage to implement reforms even when they are uncomfortable.\n\nAs I continue to write these memoirs, I hope that the lessons captured here will inform future leaders and inspire them to act decisively\u2014so that Sri Lanka, too, can write its own story of transformation\n\nSunil G Wijesinha is a Consultant on Productivity and Japanese Management Techniques\n\nAwardee of the APO Regional Award for promoting Productivity in the Asia Pacific Region\n\nRecipient of the \u201cOrder of the Rising Sun, Gold and Silver Rays\u201d from the Government of Japan.\n\nMore countries now require tobacco plain packaging and graphic health warnings on cigarette packages\n\nMaking myself heard in Parliament and NGO role in peace process\n\nLeave a Reply Cancel replyYour email address will not be published. Required fields are marked *Comment * Name *\n\nSave my name, email, and website in this browser for the next time I comment."
