    "chunk_text": "The National Trust\u2014Sri Lanka\n\nThe National Trust \u2013 Sri Lanka celebrated its 20th anniversary on November 26 at the College of Surgeons auditorium with special guests, Trustees and Members. It was an occasion to remember the unique legacy of Dr. Roland Silva, a distinguished archaeologist and architect who served as the Director of Archeology for many years and was responsible for initiating many heritage organizations in this country.\n\nExhibition on Dr. Roland Silva\u2019s work by Dr. Sagara Jayasinghe of the University of Moratuwa\n\nTrustee Dr. Kavan Ratnatunga gifting speaker Dr. Nilan Cooray with a copy of NT latest publication, The Story of Furniture in Sri Lanka\n\nIntangible Heritage Publications gold medal winner Mr. T.M Gunawardana with Mrs. Neela Silva and NT Vice President Prof. D.P Chandrasekara\n\nThis year, three organizations founded by Dr. Roland Silva, the Central Cultural Fund (CCF), ICOMOS Sri Lanka and the National Trust Sri Lanka (NT) are celebrating his legacy. The CCF and ICOMOS Sri Lanka organized in October, a national conference titled Beyond Roland Silva: Charting New Frontiers in Sri Lanka\u2019s Cultural Heritage, recalling that in 1990 in a historic and unanimous decision, Dr. Roland Silva was elected the first non-European President of the global organization ICOMOS International, serving for three consecutive terms. A special film and exhibition curated by Dr Sagara Jayasinghe of ICOMOS Sri Lanka described Dr. Roland\u2019s unique achievements and the inspiration he provides to so many young professionals to continue this work for the sake of future generations.\n\nElu3168y3r0jq_tmp_8de0c54cc25a47cc ven after retirement, Dr Roland\u2019s dedication continued through the founding of the National Trust- Sri Lanka, promoting collaboration between professionals and the public in safeguarding the nation\u2019s heritage. He launched the series of best-selling coffee table publications which today provides the foundation of financial stability for this voluntary organization."
