    "chunk_text": "Elu3168y3r0jq_tmp_8de0c54cc25a47cc ven after retirement, Dr Roland\u2019s dedication continued through the founding of the National Trust- Sri Lanka, promoting collaboration between professionals and the public in safeguarding the nation\u2019s heritage. He launched the series of best-selling coffee table publications which today provides the foundation of financial stability for this voluntary organization.\n\nA highlight of the National Trust celebration was an inspiring oration by Dr. Nilan Cooray, the Director General of the Central Cultural Fund, recalling Dr. Roland\u2019s many achievements both at the national and international levels and how today new technologies are taking heritage conservation to unprecedented heights.\n\nTrustee Kavan Ratnatunga presented Dr. Nilan Cooray with the first copy of the newest National Trust publication: The Story of Furniture in Sri Lanka.\n\nThe National Trust 20th Anniversary celebration featured the award ceremony for the open competition launched by former National Trust President Professor Nimal de Silva for publications on intangible heritage in the three languages. The jury of senior academics led by Professor D.P. Chandrasekara Senior, Vice President National Trust made the awards as follows:\n\nFirst Prize \u2013 Gold Medal, Certificate and Rs. 100,000/=, awarded to T.M. Gunawardana for his publication Urumaya Ahimi Veddo, three Second Prizes, Silver Medals Certificates and Rs. 50,000/= awarded each to Mahinda K. Wimalasiri for his publication Horanewa- The unique Symbol of Sri Lankan Traditional and Indigenous Folk Music, to Yasanjali Devika Jayathilaka for her publication Siri Laka Wedi Katha and to Hayathri Shanmuganathan for her publication Sottkalal Nilam Kadanthavarkal (Who crossed the borders with their words- review of Sri Lankan women\u2019s articles).\n\nLeave a Reply Cancel replyYour email address will not be published. Required fields are marked *Comment * Name *\n\nSave my name, email