    "chunk_size_bytes": 1620,
    "chunk_text": "Book Rview: ODYSSEY OF WAR\n\nDoctor Nadesan is a remarkable gentleman. As a Sri Lanka expatriate in Australia he practiced as a veterinary surgeon for many years. That was his profession. But quite obviously writing is his passion. He began by editing and publishing a monthly bilingual tabloid newspaper in Melbourne, and this at a time when there were few such publications linking Sri Lanka Expatriates across the vast spaces of Australia. But his literary aspirations were more ambitious; they were creative.\n\nNadesan wanted to create stories, tell stories, draw on life and events around him, both in Australia and Sri Lanka; to weave plots and narratives and through novels bring to life people, probe their loves and hates, their desires and aversions. He wanted sculpture-like, to tease out the minute but no less real feelings, desires and motives of his characters. To set them on a literary stage where they could tell the story which only he could perceive and reproduce.\n\nOver two decades he has written and published Butterfly Lake (2007) Lost in You (2013) Veterinary Vignettes and King Asoka\u2019s Veterinary Hospital (2020). Though a novel, King Asoka was faintly autobiographical. Odyssey on the other hand is a work of fiction interwoven into Sri Lanka\u2019s quarter century long civil war.\n\nHe explains that \u201cthose who have written about the War so far are those who took part in the War. Since I have never experienced war directly, the characters I have created are fictional. The novel follows them wherever they go. This is not a war novel. This is just the story of the victims of the Eelam War.\u201d"
