    "chunk_text": "Simply because you feel lonely in your old age you cannot prevent your children from going abroad. Therefore you have to find ways and means to face up to the situation. You cannot stay indoors all the time because you are on your own. You cannot go out alone and you have no one to keep company with. Lonely people are easy victims of depression. When you do not meet anyone or find no one to talk to life becomes miserable.\n\nThe epidemic is not confined to developing countries, such as Sri Lanka. Loneliness is a serious problem in developed countries as well. In major cities you find houses occupied by single parents. Their children have gone abroad for higher studies or employment. According to the Australian Loneliness Report, more than one quarter of Australians feel lonely. Even in the United States a survey has revealed that many people over 45 feel lonely. In Japan the percentage of lonely elderly people is on the increase. The situation has become acute with low birth rates of babies. The phenomenon of dying alone in Japan is known as kudokushi.\n\nYou do not need a medical expert to tell you that loneliness is injurious to health. Loneliness is linked to a range of problems, including high blood pressure, weakened immune system, cardio vascular diseases, depression and strokes. What is more, lonely people are likely to die prematurely. Some experts compare loneliness to smoking 15 cigarettes a day!"
