    "md5": "b8d746a5f05d8af5a6472f9bf1644d81",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1703,
    "chunk_text": "You do not need a medical expert to tell you that loneliness is injurious to health. Loneliness is linked to a range of problems, including high blood pressure, weakened immune system, cardio vascular diseases, depression and strokes. What is more, lonely people are likely to die prematurely. Some experts compare loneliness to smoking 15 cigarettes a day!\n\nIf you feel lonely, try to have more connections with others. Supporting others is a two-way process. You support others and they will support you in return. Not only lonely people but also others can benefit from having more connections and support from others. Everybody is mutually helped up to the next level and you will see a knock-on effect. One such knock-on effect has been a 17 percent fall in emergency hospital admissions in Frome.\n\nIf you feel a sense of purpose in life, it is much easier to look after yourself. When you feel isolated, you do not feel that your life has any value. You should have something to share with others. It can be wealth, knowledge or skills. Some retired teachers give free tuition to children who cannot afford to pay tutors. It can be a small step in the right direction in a world of increasing loneliness.\n\nPhilosophically, loneliness has both a sort of sadness, sorrow and a very deep peace and silence. Everything depends on how you look at it. Most people feel uncomfortable when they begin to live alone. However, unless you live alone you will never be acquainted with your own being. What is more, you will never come to know who you are. When you are always occupied with worldly affairs you begin to live on the surface. If you live on the surface, your relationships cannot have any depth."
