    "chunk_text": "Philosophically, loneliness has both a sort of sadness, sorrow and a very deep peace and silence. Everything depends on how you look at it. Most people feel uncomfortable when they begin to live alone. However, unless you live alone you will never be acquainted with your own being. What is more, you will never come to know who you are. When you are always occupied with worldly affairs you begin to live on the surface. If you live on the surface, your relationships cannot have any depth.\n\nThe lesson we have to learn from her is that when you have emotionally supportive relationships your biological stress is very low. However, it may not be wise for you to invite close relatives to live with you. Sometimes such relationships may drive you crazy. It is the paucity of intimate relationships that lead to loneliness. Biologically, the brain\u2019s daily manufacture of new neurons will continue into your old age at a slower rate. According to certain surveys, women appear to be more biologically vulnerable to the ups and downs of life than men.\n\nPsychologically, if you feel lonely, you need social support. University students, who do not have many friends, will feel lonely. This kind of loneliness can be psychologically painful. It can have a negative effect on the immune system. A recent study shows a clear relationship between loneliness and psychological stress. Therefore university students are encouraged to be active in community service, clubs and sports to develop social networks.\n\nPeople are lonely because they build walls instead of bridges.\u2013  Joseph Fort Newton\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s vanishing snakes: Scientists warn policy paralysis is driving a silent crisis\n\nLeave a Reply Cancel replyYour email address will not be published. Required fields are marked *Comment * Name *\n\nSave my name, email, and website in this browser for the next time I comment."
