    "chunk_text": "Environmental Impact Assessments (EIAs) rarely examine the fine-scale habitat needs of snakes. Many species\u2014especially burrowing forms\u2014can vanish from a site without ever being counted.\n\n\u201cA single road or tea expansion can wipe out an entire endemic population, but current assessments don\u2019t reflect that.\u201d\n\nEven the rules that do exist suffer from chronic non-implementation.\n\n\u201cWe are losing snakes to chemicals and pollutants long before anyone realises what happened. But monitoring is almost nonexistent,\u201d Karunarathna told The Island.\n\nHe added despite snakes being legally protected, killings in villages remain widespread, with no systematic response mechanism or structured data collection.\n\nNo national programme exists for safe relocation, community-level identification training, or village-based rapid responders\u2014even though such systems have been successful in India and Nepal.\n\nWhile Sri Lanka\u2019s climate policies talk broadly about biodiversity, they rarely address species-specific vulnerabilities\u2014especially for cold-blooded animals.\n\n\u201cSnakes are extremely temperature-sensitive. Climate shifts affect reproduction, hibernation, and prey cycles. We need predictive models and adaptation plans, but none exist,\u201d Kandambi stresses.\n\nSri Lanka has no national research agenda on the impacts of extreme weather, warming trends, or rainfall anomalies on reptile populations\u2014even though the country ranks among the world\u2019s most climate-vulnerable.\n\nDespite their ecological importance, snakes remain one of the least-studied major groups.\n\n\u201cWe don\u2019t even fully know where some endemic species occur. Without baseline data, how can we protect them?\u201d Karunarathna asks.\n\nMeanwhile, taxonomic work\u2014often funded by small international grants\u2014has outpaced local conservation policy by nearly a decade. Sri Lankan research teams have described several new species recently, yet none have been incorporated into national red lists or land-use planning frameworks."
