    "chunk_index": 2,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "a7d6ce1da4fee0d203b89d50ae395e8c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1739,
    "chunk_text": "Meanwhile, taxonomic work\u2014often funded by small international grants\u2014has outpaced local conservation policy by nearly a decade. Sri Lankan research teams have described several new species recently, yet none have been incorporated into national red lists or land-use planning frameworks.\n\nWhile the Department of Agriculture has pesticide guidelines, field scientists say enforcement is almost nonexistent.\n\n\u201cRunoff from agrochemicals doesn\u2019t just kill pests\u2014it changes entire food webs. Frogs decline, rodents increase, and snakes either starve or wander into villages, increasing conflict,\u201d Kandambi explains.\n\nStill, the country has no regulation compelling large plantations to maintain ecological buffers around sensitive habitats.\n\nKarunarathna and Kandambi say Sri Lanka urgently needs a National Snake Conservation Policy\u2014a structured, modern, science-based framework. They outline the following priorities:\n\nInclude lesser-known and newly described species, strengthen penalties, and restrict development in critical habitats.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s snakes do not roar, charge, or dominate the landscape. Their decline is quiet, often invisible. But the consequences\u2014ecological imbalance, rodent surges, collapsing food webs\u2014will be anything but quiet.\n\n\u201cIf policy does not change soon, we will lose species before we even discover them,\u201d Karunarathna warns.\n\nThe question now is whether policymakers will listen\u2014or allow yet another chapter of Sri Lanka\u2019s natural heritage to disappear silently \u2026 beneath the leaves.\n\nLeave a Reply Cancel replyYour email address will not be published. Required fields are marked *Comment * Name *\n\nSave my name, email, and website in this browser for the next time I comment."
