    "md5": "81e7335e3aa2967b348ac6456208b5f3",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1977,
    "chunk_text": "It was heartening to see two choppers from Vikrant, India\u2019s pride, braving inclement weather and doing sorties to save lives in the disaster-stricken areas of this country. Their deployment has stood many disaster victims in good stead. This humanitarian gesture has cast India, often perceived as the Orwellian Big Brother, in the exalted image of Loku Aiya or Periya Anna, the affectionate Sri Lankan term for the caring eldest brother.\n\nMeanwhile, climate change and the alarming increase in natural disasters the world over have demonstrated the interconnectedness of nations and the shared vulnerability of humanity. No country is safe however big it may be. Nature is no respecter of the military might and economic prowess of any country.\n\nOne could argue that nature, like the Grim Reaper, is a great leveller. Numerous tragic weather events in many countries during the past several decades bears this out. They include Hurricane Katrina (2005) and the California wildfires (2024) in the US. It is doubtful whether there is any country that has been free from such disasters.\n\nSadly, US President Donald Trump is a dyed-in-the-wool Climate Change naysayer. He is prominent among the leaders who question, deny, or express scepticism about human-caused climate change and its impacts. How many more weather disasters the world must endure before they abandon their climate denialism and come to terms with reality?\n\nDire warnings of far worse weather disasters in the next few years should jolt all nations into devising ways and means of facing the unfolding Climate Emergency collectively. There is no defence against nature\u2019s fury. At this rate, the day may not be far off when all countries, big or small, are left with no alternative but to cut down on their defence budgets, allocate more funds for disaster management and even resort to measures such as repurposing their military vehicles to carry out rescue missions and provide relief to disaster victims."
  },
