    "md5": "1dab0ce7b1f09acbd20c106e05864add",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1918,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka facing post-deluge multi-billion rupee recovery bill\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s already-stretched public finances are bracing for another significant setback, with senior officials confirming that the economic losses from the recent floods will far exceed initial expectations.\n\nEarly assessments point to a multibillion-rupee recovery bill that will pressure budget allocations, delay development projects, and tighten the government\u2019s fiscal space over the coming months.\n\nA senior Environment Ministry official, speaking on condition of anonymity as final assessments are underway, said the scale of damage to agriculture and irrigation alone had prompted an urgent request for more than Rs. 30 billion in restoration funds.\n\nHe added: \u201cThis is not a routine seasonal impact. The destruction to cultivated land, irrigation channels, and rural infrastructure is unprecedented in recent years,\u201d the official said.\n\n\u201cWe have submitted a consolidated request to restore paddy fields, rehabilitate minor tanks, repair canals, and stabilize affected ecosystems.\u201d\n\nAccording to the Ministry, more than half a million hectares of cultivated land were inundated or washed away, raising fresh concerns about food security, supply chain disruptions, and the need for increased imports during the first quarter of 2026. Officials noted that re-cultivation support, fertilizer subsidies, and emergency interventions will be essential to avert a domestic food shortage.\n\nThe Disaster Management Centre (DMC), meanwhile, confirmed that its own estimates\u2014still preliminary\u2014show that the national recovery bill will rise sharply once the full impact on housing, roads, electricity networks, and public assets is tallied.\n\nA senior DMC official told The Island Financial Review that the immediate relief and emergency response phase alone had already consumed a significant portion of the 2025 disaster-relief allocation."
  },
