    "chunk_text": "Flood disaster taking toll on share market\n\nThe CSE was trading down yesterday shortly after open, as the full extent of the disaster following Cyclone Ditwah was becoming more apparent.\n\nThe reason being that investors were concerned that economic development will slow down and cost of production also would increase.\n\nThe All Share Price Index was down by 690.77 points, while the S&P SL20 declined by 162.65 points.\n\n\u201cThere is some panic selling, which we have seen in the past, too, when starting trading.\n\nThe selling was seen across the board. But the market has recovered a bit from open, analysts said.\n\nTurnover stood at Rs 5.24 billion with seven crossings. Those crossings were reported in HNB where 300,000 shares crossed to the tune of Rs 118.5 million, its shares traded at Rs 395, Hemas 2.6 million shares crossed to the tune of Rs 90 million; its shares traded at Rs 35, Access Engineering one million shares crossed for Rs 78 million; ts shares traded at Rs 74, JKH t2 million shares crossed to the tune of Rs 43.8 million; its shares sold at Rs 21.80, Commercial Bank 104,000 shares crossed to the tune of Rs 20.9 million;its shares traded at Rs 200, Chevron Lubricants 115,000 shares crossed for Rs 20.7 million; its shares sold at Rs 180 and Colombo Dockyard 45500 shares crossed to the tune of Rs 440.\n\nIn the retail market top seven companies that mainly contributed to the turnover were; Access Engineering Rs 421 million (5.7 million shares traded), Sampath Bank Rs 250 million (1.8 million shares traded), HNB Rs 202 million (511,000 shares traded), Commercial Bank Rs 157 million (835,000 shares traded), Tokyo Cement Rs 156 million (1.7 million shares traded), Sierra Cables Rs 139 million (4.3 million shares traded) and JKH Rs 137 million (6.3 million shares traded). During the day 236 million shares volumes changed hands in 56142 transactions."
