    "md5": "5a287ee8e705385bf8b90fb2436b8dc7",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1553,
    "chunk_text": "Ditwah: An unusual cyclone\n\nThe increase in the temperature of the equatorial ocean is caused by climate change\n\nNormally a cyclone develops at a minimum distance of 500 km from the equator, but Cyclone Ditwah broke this norm; it began in the southern sea, near Sri Lanka, located about 650 km north of the equator.\n\nCyclone Ditwah, which swept through Sri Lanka, has caused serious damages to the country. The torrential rainfall has saturated the entire country and the subsequent accumulation of surface water in river basins, triggering massive floods and landslides due to soil saturation, which caused a series of disasters. This unprecedented disaster has paralyzed the entire country at the moment. However, it is impos sible to predict the development of hurricanes, months in advance, so there may be very little time to prepare once it is known.\n\nAlthough cyclones have formed earlier in Sri Lanka, this is the first time in the history that a cyclone of this severity has occurred. That is why this cyclone is so special. Normally, cyclones do not form very close to or on the equator. The main reason for this is that the storm does not receive the necessary energy to rotate. That energy is obtained from the Coriolis force. Since that force is negligible near the equator, the pressure depressions do not receive the enough energy to rotate. Normally a cyclone develops at a minimum distance of 500 km from the equator. But Ditwah broke this norm and it began in the southern sea near Sri Lanka, located about 650 km north of the equator."
