    "chunk_size_bytes": 1304,
    "chunk_text": "Although cyclones have formed earlier in Sri Lanka, this is the first time in the history that a cyclone of this severity has occurred. That is why this cyclone is so special. Normally, cyclones do not form very close to or on the equator. The main reason for this is that the storm does not receive the necessary energy to rotate. That energy is obtained from the Coriolis force. Since that force is negligible near the equator, the pressure depressions do not receive the enough energy to rotate. Normally a cyclone develops at a minimum distance of 500 km from the equator. But Ditwah broke this norm and it began in the southern sea near Sri Lanka, located about 650 km north of the equator.\n\nIt is very clear that these cyclone conditions that have occurred in the three ocean regions of the Bay of Bengal, the Strait of Malacca and the South China Sea, within a period of about a week, are giving the countries in the tropical zone a chance to think about a new trend developing in the weather conditions. It seems that we will have to find out why a cyclone, with such a certain intensity, develops unusually in a cyclone free zone. If this situation starts to emerge again and again, it must be said that it is impossible to prevent the countries in this region from being in danger continuously."
