    "md5": "849403087d5128a613a1fb868f4780e6",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1002,
    "chunk_text": "The SJB argued in Parliament yesterday that the Meteorological Department and the Irrigation Department had warned of the possibility of a cyclone, a couple of weeks before the occurrence of the catastrophic weather event. It said the water in major reservoirs should have been released gradually to obviate the need for opening their sluice gates suddenly causing floods downstream. It cited floods in Gampola as an example, and blamed the government for the lives lost.\n\nIt never rains but it pours. Cyclones of the severity of Ditwah are rare phenomena in regions close to the equator, but climate change has created conditions for the formation of them, as Dr. Pathmakumara Jayasingha has pointed out in an article published in this newspaper today. So, Sri Lanka will have to remain vigilant and prepared to meet more such disasters. The government would do better to heed future warnings about catastrophic weather events, inform Parliament and adopt a consensual approach to disaster management."
