    "md5": "5a845b9abfc23713743caa34b195863a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1662,
    "chunk_text": "Telecom operators, partners accelerate restoration of connectivity after Cyclone Ditwah\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s national telecommunication operators, in close coordination with Government authorities, are intensifying round-the-clock recovery efforts to reconnect communities battered by severe flooding and landslides triggered by Cyclone Ditwah since 27 November.\n\nThe disaster has claimed 334 lives, left nearly 400 people missing or unaccounted for, and affected more than 1.3 million individuals nationwide, with rising river levels prompting evacuation warnings for low-lying areas from the National Disaster Management Centre.\n\nWidespread power outages and damage to backbone optical-fibre networks have disrupted a significant portion of the national network, causing major service interruptions, particularly in Colombo, Gampaha, Kalutara, Kandy, Ratnapura, and other Central Province districts. These outages have isolated families and hampered emergency coordination, but all operators have extended free mobile connectivity until today (2 December) to support affected households and relief operations.\n\nOn 28 November, the Telecommunications Regulatory Commission of Sri Lanka (TRCSL) convened emergency meetings with the country\u2019s major operators, Dialog Axiata, SLT-Mobitel, and Hutch, urging seamless collaboration with the Ceylon Electricity Board for rapid deployment of generators and backup power solutions. Field teams are now prioritising backbone repairs, site stabilisation, and temporary power installations, targeting substantial network recovery so that voice, data, and emergency lines can serve public safety and family communication needs."
