    "num": "dailyftlk-c69934b4",
    "date_str": "2025-12-03",
    "description": "WHO provides $ 175,000 emergency funds to scale up health response in cyclone-hit Sri Lanka",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/news/WHO-provides-175-000-emergency-funds-to-scale-up-health-response-in-cyclone-hit-Sri-Lanka/56-785148",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1764717303.0,
    "chunk_id": "2025-12-03-dailyftlk-c69934b4-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "7eba95fb099958facc7c09c12cb0ccb5",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1936,
    "chunk_text": "WHO provides $ 175,000 emergency funds to scale up health response in cyclone-hit Sri Lanka\n\nAs Sri Lanka faces rapidly worsening conditions triggered by a cyclonic storm, the World Health Organisation yesterday provided $ 175,000 as emergency funds to support essential health services, while continuing to work with national authorities and humanitarian partners to reach life-saving interventions to the millions of affected people across the country.\n\nWHO Representative-Designate to Sri Lanka Dr. Rajesh Pandav said, \u201cThe funds will be used for rapid response teams to support essential health services for the affected communities, and for strengthening health information management and surveillance, key for timely detection of disease outbreaks to facilitate appropriate response.\u201d\n\nCyclone Ditwah made landfall in Sri Lanka on 28 November, causing unprecedented flooding, landslides, and major infrastructure damage across the country. Multiple casualties, significant displacement, and severe disruption to essential services and livelihoods have been reported countrywide.\n\nWHO funding, partly from WHO South-East Asia Regional Health Emergency Fund (SEARHEF), supports operational costs for mobilisation and deployment of Rapid Response medical and public health teams which will deliver trauma/first aid on site, refer individuals for hospital care, attend to pregnant women, children, the elderly, among others.\n\nThe Rapid Response medical and public health teams will conduct medical stocktaking, and assess medical needs, water quality, sanitation, food safety, and environmental health risks.\n\nAs lead agency on health sector response, WHO is supporting a Joint Rapid Needs Assessment rolled out by national authorities and partners, to guide Government-led response.\n\nWHO teams have been working closely with the national authorities and partners since the disaster struck the country, for a coordinated response."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
