    "md5": "e8c7c7111407d22c4b057ee076279215",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1677,
    "chunk_text": "The Stockholm International Peace Research Institute (SIPRI) disclosed some of the most thought-provoking statistics in this regard. It is on record that revenues derived from weapons and military services by the world\u2019s 100 largest arms-producing companies \u2018reached a record $679 billion in 2024\u2019. Companies based in Europe and the US were seen as leading from the front in the globe-wide revenue-extraction resulting from the escalation of such arms sales. Besides, the combined arms revenues of weapons manufacturers featuring among the topmost of these enterprises grew by 3.8 percent in 2024 to reach $334 billion, it is reported.\n\nWhile it is plain to see how arms manufacturers would stand to gain from this situation, the countries concerned would, on the other hand, lose badly if the operations of their arms industries are in any way hampered. For one, the relevant states would lose tax revenue as a consequence and for another, the work force concerned would be rendered unemployed. There is then too much at stake for the big military powers.\n\nAccordingly, a blind eye will be turned on climate change and its consequences by the world\u2019s most influential states with the consequence that environment related deliberations in major international forums, including the relevant UN agencies, would be gravely hampered. COP30 has just borne this out. Major decisions in the latter talks were no doubt taken, but nothing concrete was agreed upon to hold the major Western powers and their arms manufacturers in any way accountable for the climate harm being visited on the developing world as a consequence of their profit-oriented, self-empowering operations."
