    "chunk_text": "Accordingly, a blind eye will be turned on climate change and its consequences by the world\u2019s most influential states with the consequence that environment related deliberations in major international forums, including the relevant UN agencies, would be gravely hampered. COP30 has just borne this out. Major decisions in the latter talks were no doubt taken, but nothing concrete was agreed upon to hold the major Western powers and their arms manufacturers in any way accountable for the climate harm being visited on the developing world as a consequence of their profit-oriented, self-empowering operations.\n\nThe above questions and more point to the necessity for the global South in particular to acquire the capability and power to work unchallenged towards meeting their just needs in the environment field and other areas of vital national and international interest. In other words, the South would need to re-visit the issues in the North-South Dialogue of the sixties and seventies. It would need to work out as to how the international power balance could be changed in its favour, since currently it is the North that possesses the bulk of global power and influence.\n\nThe developing world holds the majority in the UN General Assembly and some allied institutions but without substantive executive power it cannot do much to mould the current global power balance in its favour. Nor would a strong alignment with the BRICS grouping prove beneficial because within the BRICS fold are some international law violators who very much depend on their armed might.\n\nOne choice is for the majority of the Southern countries to align with states within their grouping who are leading democracies and notable \u2018Swing States\u2019; that is, democratic countries that wield tangible power and influence in the councils of the world. Two such states are India and South Africa. The latter\u2019s voice cannot be easily overlooked by the big powers since they wield decisive influence in international affairs."
