    "md5": "f3e5d002b3c0edf25bfc344b00128d2d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2049,
    "chunk_text": "Chaos at Miss Universe 2025:Winner predecided?\n\nThis seems to be a common scene at beauty pageants where there are allegations that the results are fixed.\n\nIt is happening here, as well \u2013 where beauty pageants are popping up like mushrooms \u2013 and I strongly believe the Ministry of Women and Child Affairs should step in and bring some order in the setup here.\n\nI\u2019m told that some of these pageants have turned out to be money-spinners for the organisers, with contestants having to pay huge fees to participate, etc., etc.\n\nYes, the Miss Universe 2025 pageant, in Thailand, ended in heated controversy after Mexico\u2019s F\u00e1tima Bosch was crowned the winner.\n\nAccording to reports, there were loud protests, inside the venue, with fans chanting \u201ccooking show\u201d in anger over the result; \u201ccooking show,\u201d in pageant slang, means that the crown was \u201cpre-cooked\u201d or predecided\u2014essentially an allegation of a fixed result.\n\nThailand\u2019s Veena Praveenar Singh finished as first runner-up, while Stephany Abasali of Venezuela was placed second, Ahtisa Manalo of the Philippines came third, and Olivia Yac\u00e9 of Cote d\u2019lvoire secured fourth place.\n\nThe final outcome triggered a wave of frustration from pageant supporters worldwide, many of whom accused the competition of being predetermined, while a former judge claimed the result was prearranged.\n\nFuel was added to the drama when Omar Harfouch, a Lebanese-French musician, and a Miss Universe 2025 judge, resigned shortly before the final, publicly alleging that Mexico\u2019s victory was orchestrated for business reasons.\n\nHarfouch claimed, on Instagram, that he had already told HBO America (an American premium television network)\u2014a full 24 hours before the final\u2014that Mexico would win. He said this was because Ra\u00fal Rocha, the owner of the Miss Universe Organisation, had business ties with F\u00e1tima\u2019s father.\n\nHe further alleged that Rocha, and his son, contacted him in Dubai to personally ask for his vote, telling him directly that \u201cit will be good for our business.\u201d"
