    "description": "Binance contributes Rs. 61.6 m for flood relief in Sri Lanka",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/business/Binance-contributes-Rs-61-6-m-for-flood-relief-in-Sri-Lanka/34-785192",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1764807962.0,
    "chunk_id": "2025-12-04-dailyftlk-0b5375f5-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "b99a6d06b0999e93f9ca03ce0b9dba89",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1648,
    "chunk_text": "Binance contributes Rs. 61.6 m for flood relief in Sri Lanka\n\nFunds directed through Sri Lanka Red Cross Society to support communities impacted by Cyclone Ditwah\n\nIn response to the devastating impact of Cyclone Ditwah, which has triggered severe rains and flooding across Sri Lanka, Binance Charity, the philanthropic arm of the global blockchain leader Binance, has pledged Rs. 61.6 million ($ 200,000) to support urgent humanitarian and early-recovery efforts. The donation will be directed through the Sri Lanka Red Cross Society (SLRCS) to bolster immediate relief for communities affected by the widespread destruction.\n\nCyclone Ditwah has disrupted the lives of over one million people nationwide, submerging homes, damaging critical infrastructure, and cutting off access to essential services. According to the Disaster Management Centre, nearly 200,000 individuals are currently taking refuge in over 1000 temporary shelters after being displaced by the heavy rainfall and flooding. Authorities have noted that power, water and communications will be gradually restored over the next few days, after transmission lines and transformers were swept away by the cyclone and resulting floods.\n\nBinance Charity\u2019s contribution will be allocated toward life-saving relief essentials, including food supplies, clean drinking water, hygiene and dignity kits, medical supplies, emergency medical assistance, and temporary shelter materials as well as providing cash grants for the most vulnerable communities. This support aims to ease the immediate burden on displaced families and help accelerate recovery efforts in the hardest-hit districts."
  },
