    "chunk_text": "Although the official death toll from Cyclone Ditwah stands at nearly 390, this number tells only a part of the story. Entire villages have been buried under landslides, cut off from rescue teams, and erased from registries. Survivors speak of hamlets swallowed whole, families lost without a trace, and communities that exist now only in memory. The true toll is likely to exceed ten times the reported figure.\n\nThis is not unfamiliar terrain. When floods claimed hundreds of lives and left tens of thousands homeless in 2016, the nation mourned, rebuilt, and promised reform. Yet nine years later, the same vulnerabilities remain with fragile infrastructure, poor drainage, and a disaster management system that reacts only after tragedy strikes. Cyclone Ditwah has exposed, once again, the cost of unpreparedness.\n\nCyclone Ditwah did not arrive without warning. On 12 and again on 20 November, international agencies and broadcasters, including the BBC, flagged the risk of severe flooding across Sri Lanka. The signals were clear, the science was sound, and the time to act was available. Yet the state remained deaf to the alarms and blind to the risks.\n\nThe BBC\u2019s report of 20 November categorically mentioned \u201ccyclone\u201d \u2013 not a passing storm, but heavy rain up to 500 mm and a cyclone. The language was unambiguous and the threat was unmistakable. Those who watched it saw the danger spelled out in plain terms. Yet the Government failed to act, treating a categorical cyclone warning as if it were routine weather.\n\nPreparedness was minimal. Evacuation orders were delayed, drainage systems were left clogged, and relief stocks were poorly positioned. By the time the waters rose, the Government was scrambling to respond rather than preventing the worst. This reactive posture turned a natural hazard into a national tragedy."
  },
