    "chunk_size_bytes": 1851,
    "chunk_text": "Preparedness was minimal. Evacuation orders were delayed, drainage systems were left clogged, and relief stocks were poorly positioned. By the time the waters rose, the Government was scrambling to respond rather than preventing the worst. This reactive posture turned a natural hazard into a national tragedy.\n\nThis deafness to warnings is not new. In 2019, India\u2019s RAW notified the Yahapalanaya Government under Maithripala Sirisena of imminent threats before the Easter Sunday attacks. The warnings were ignored, and the courts later fined Sirisena and others for negligence. At the time, the Opposition made hell out of this lapse in Parliament, condemning it as unforgivable. Today, those same voices sit in Government, yet Cyclone Ditwah shows the same deafness and blindness. Warnings were clear, but action was absent.\n\nInternational donors have pledged support, but even here the limitations are stark. India, China, the United States, Australia, Japan, have all committed humanitarian grants. Yet these grants will arrive in kind, not in cash \u2014 food, medicine, tents, and equipment \u2014 and only if donors are satisfied that the goods reach the affected communities without infiltration or diversion. This reflects a deep mistrust of Sri Lanka\u2019s relief channels, where past experiences of leakage and politicisation have eroded confidence. This mistrust is not unfounded\n\nEven technical protocols were disregarded. Engineers of the Ceylon Electricity Board (CEB) confirm that when rainfall exceeding 200 mm is predicted in reservoir catchment areas, the regulation is to open the gates in advance to prevent overflow and downstream flooding. Yet during Ditwah, gates remained closed until it was too late. The result was catastrophic as reservoirs spilled suddenly, amplifying floods and sweeping away homes to compound the destruction."
