    "chunk_id": "2025-12-06-dailyftlk-891e8a0b-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "74e1a99fc60f491c920c2c9e2d7db3c4",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 901,
    "chunk_text": "SLT\u2011Mobitel calls for public support to keep Sri Lanka connected\n\nSLT\u2011Mobitel urgently requests the public not to remove or cut the SLT-Mobitel Fibre and Copper cables that may be lying on the ground following the recent extreme weather conditions. These cables are operational even when fallen, and several service disruptions have already been reported throughout multiple cities due to their removal or damage. Safeguarding these cables is vital to ensure uninterrupted communication during the crisis.\n\nAt the same time, SLT\u2011Mobitel extends its gratitude and seeks public cooperation in supporting the dedicated workforce, who are tirelessly helping to restore normal life by clearing roads, removing fallen trees, and tackling debris and other obstacles. Their efforts are essential to national recovery, and community support is imperative to help them carry out the work more effectively."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2025-12-06-dailyftlk-a81299e2",
    "num": "dailyftlk-a81299e2",
    "date_str": "2025-12-06",
    "description": "Apparel sector activates industry-wide relief, recovery effort",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/business/Apparel-sector-activates-industry-wide-relief-recovery-effort/34-785309",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1764963554.0,
    "chunk_id": "2025-12-06-dailyftlk-a81299e2-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "3c41f38aa77717ff3d85d9f8b150f8bf",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1704,
    "chunk_text": "Apparel sector activates industry-wide relief, recovery effort\n\nThe Joint Apparel Association Forum (JAAF) has announced that the industry has activated a coordinated response to safeguard communities affected by the severe weather conditions across Sri Lanka.\n\nWith reports of flooding, landslides and access disruptions in several districts, JAAF confirmed that employee safety and wellbeing remain the sector\u2019s highest priority.\n\nJAAF is working closely with all member organisations to identify affected employees, assess immediate needs, and ensure timely assistance. Most factories across the country have resumed normal operations, with only a few isolated pockets still facing access and other infrastructure-related challenges. Across the country, companies are already providing emergency support, including food, clothing, temporary shelter, transport assistance, and other essential supplies for displaced workers, their families and the local community.\n\nTo streamline relief coordination, JAAF has established focal points for member companies to report affected communities and to share district-level impact updates. This mechanism allows faster mobilisation of resources and ensures no employee or family is overlooked.\n\nJAAF is also in active communication with Government authorities, providing real-time information on affected employees and supporting national relief efforts in regions where access remains difficult.\n\nThe Association notes \u201cOur industry\u2019s resilience comes from our people. We are committed to standing with every employee affected by this disaster, and we are working with our members and the Government to ensure immediate recovery and long-term support.\u201d"
  },
