    "chunk_size_bytes": 1861,
    "chunk_text": "I found these statistics retrieved by googling. \u201cThe Yangtze-Huai or China River Floods 1931 \u2013 3.7 million dead \u2013 was the world\u2019s deadliest natural disaster in the 20th century. Other disasters are graded thus: tsunami 42%, earthquake 35%, hurricane 33%, wildfires 30%, heatwave or drought 6%, blizzard 4%, mudslide 4%.\n\nA monastic who names himself in his videos as English Buddhist Monk, living near Bentota, released a video named \u201cCyclone and Buddhism\u201d. He gave an account of his days during the height of Cyclone Dilwah, continuing his daily practice of going pinna patha \u2013 daily alms round for his one meal a day \u2013 even when it was raining sheets of water. He walks barefoot, thus braving centipedes, even snakes in the water. He had to climb over a fallen tree, the fallen tree being five feet above the ground, which he did. People in the locality were full of loving care but he insisted on the food-begging round. He found the much loved trees in his garden fallen. All these were mentioned to emphasize the basic truths of Buddhism: impermanence; inability for us to control certain events; futility of clinging and the compassion of communities and uniting for the common good and safety during crises.\n\nVery different to how politicians acted recently. The Opposition raised a furor accusing the government of not taking due notice of weather warnings. One suggestion was that wevas and reservoirs should have been carefully made to release water in view of the impending cyclone. But its path could suddenly veer from its forecast path. Then what if there is no rain? In times of national crisis, MPS should all unite, not squabble and promote individual clout: as an ex minister had gone on a food distribution campaign with his TV cameras. The affected victims had booed him off. What heartless opportunists \u2013 most politicians."
  },
